1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
This is Daily Frontier Papers.

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:09,600
Today's paper comes from Brown University, New York University, and AMI Labs by Randall Balastrero and Jan Lacoon.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:14,640
The title Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis with JPAs.

4
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,080
It takes on an old question.

5
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:22,480
If you train one model only on images and another only on text, do they end up with the same picture of the world?

6
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,892
The answer here is yes, if you choose the right positive pairs.

7
00:00:26,446 --> 00:00:30,160
And that scaling up next token prediction is a very inefficient way to get there.

8
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:36,560
Some background, an influential earlier paper observed that vision models and language models grow more similar as they scale.

9
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:40,560
It proposed the Platonic Representation Hypothesis, or PRH.

10
00:00:41,038 --> 00:00:45,840
Models of different modalities converge toward one shared statistical model of reality.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,320
The trouble is that it has stayed an observation.

12
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:55,760
For the self-supervised objectives people actually use, JEPAs in particular, there's no proof and no prediction of when it holds.

13
00:00:55,886 --> 00:00:58,960
So the main lever has been to make models and data bigger.

14
00:00:59,150 --> 00:00:59,920
There were hints.

15
00:01:00,142 --> 00:01:04,640
Word embeddings trained separately on different languages turned out to differ by just a rotation.

16
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:14,320
On the theory side, the original PRH argument rests on an idealized contrastive learner, and the closest proof the authors know of covers deep linear networks.

17
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:18,560
Plenty of work also aligns two existing embedding spaces after the fact.

18
00:01:18,734 --> 00:01:20,720
This paper asks a different question.

19
00:01:21,134 --> 00:01:24,560
Which training objective makes encoders alignable in the first place?

20
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:31,280
First, what a JEPA is a joint embedding predictive architecture, a family of self-supervised methods.

21
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,660
You give the model two inputs it should treat as the same thing.

22
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:41,840
A positive pair, pull their embeddings together, and add an anti-collapse term so everything doesn't land on one point.

23
00:01:42,030 --> 00:01:46,320
The key insight is that the PRH is really a statement about positive pairs.

24
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:52,036
If the positives are two observations of one scene, say two captions of the same photo.

25
00:01:52,526 --> 00:01:56,400
The authors call them good views, and the representation belongs to the world.

26
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:02,916
If there are two corruptions of one observation, like one sentence with different words masked, those are bad views.

27
00:02:03,278 --> 00:02:05,360
And the representation belongs to the modality.

28
00:02:05,454 --> 00:02:06,800
That's the left half of figure one.

29
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:13,360
With good views, an image jepa and a text jepa land on the same embedding up to one rotation.

30
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:14,788
Below are the bad views.

31
00:02:15,182 --> 00:02:18,720
A masked word or a prefix and its next token.

32
00:02:18,894 --> 00:02:22,320
The first result is what the authors call the platonic representation theorem.

33
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:27,920
Take a finite world with each modality rendering its states one to one.

34
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:39,280
If every modality shares the same distribution of positive pairs and the optimum is unique up to rotation, then every modality learns the same embedding of the world up to one rotation each.

35
00:02:39,534 --> 00:02:42,720
The proof is short, a one-to-one rendering just changes coordinates.

36
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:46,880
So each modality's training problem is the same problem written in a different coordinate system.

37
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:54,160
They also show that JPAs with the whitening style constraint have optima unique up to rotation as long as there's a gap between eigenvalues.

38
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:57,840
In practice, modalities won't share exactly the same positives.

39
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,960
The second theorem bounds the misalignment by two things.

40
00:03:01,262 --> 00:03:07,440
How different the graphs drawn by the two sets of positives are, and how well separated the top of the spectrum is.

41
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:14,880
On 300 randomly generated small worlds, the error always stayed below the bound, at 18% of it at most.

42
00:03:15,086 --> 00:03:17,440
The third result explains why bad views hurt.

43
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:25,680
Every observation carries a nuisance of its own modality, like which words a caption happened to use, or the angle a photo was taken from.

44
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,260
Bad view positives share that nuisance.

45
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:35,520
And when they keep more of it than of the scene, the encoder spends dimensions on it, and no rotation can line those up with the other modality.

46
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,640
Then comes the step that matters most for NLP.

47
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,160
The authors show that next token prediction has the form of a Jepa.

48
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:48,400
The context is one side, the next token is the other, and the softmax normalizer plays the anti-collapse role.

49
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:56,000
Its positives are a prefix and its continuation inside one document, which share that document's wording and style.

50
00:03:56,240 --> 00:04:00,360
So in the author's framing, it's a bad view Jepa.

51
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:04,880
They stress that this step is heuristic, a prediction to test rather than a corollary.

52
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:05,600
Look at this figure.

53
00:04:05,774 --> 00:04:10,080
Good views connect every description of a scene and never another scene.

54
00:04:10,254 --> 00:04:15,920
Next token prediction makes narrow links within a scene and spills across scenes through frequent tokens.

55
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:20,080
The authors read this as the reason language models align with vision mostly through scale.

56
00:04:20,270 --> 00:04:22,000
The setup is deliberately uniform.

57
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:26,276
Image and text encoders are trained separately with the same Legepa objective.

58
00:04:26,894 --> 00:04:30,448
On Flickr 30K, five captions per image.

59
00:04:30,638 --> 00:04:37,488
The text side is a 33 million parameter BERT trained from scratch, and no encoder ever sees the other modality.

60
00:04:37,568 --> 00:04:40,768
There are three text variants that differ only in their positives.

61
00:04:40,928 --> 00:04:42,768
Bad views take spans of one caption.

62
00:04:42,862 --> 00:04:45,328
Good views take different captions of the same image.

63
00:04:45,518 --> 00:04:50,048
And the same BERT, make causal, trained with next token prediction.

64
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:54,084
The headline metric fit a single rotation between the two spaces.

65
00:04:54,542 --> 00:04:57,408
Then check how often an image retrieves its own caption first.

66
00:04:57,646 --> 00:04:58,848
Table 1 has the results.

67
00:04:59,054 --> 00:05:04,208
Good views retrieve the right caption first, 4.4% of the time, 44 times chance.

68
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:08,288
Bad views get 2.7, next token prediction 2.1.

69
00:05:08,462 --> 00:05:11,328
The paper says that ranking holds in every seed.

70
00:05:11,502 --> 00:05:12,208
A key control.

71
00:05:12,750 --> 00:05:16,608
Train next token prediction on two captions of the same image joined together.

72
00:05:16,768 --> 00:05:20,048
It barely moves from 2.1 to 2.3.

73
00:05:20,208 --> 00:05:25,888
The authors conclude that the gain comes from the objective itself, not from knowing which captions go together.

74
00:05:26,062 --> 00:05:26,688
Then scale.

75
00:05:26,768 --> 00:05:36,768
The text eye moves to web data, recap data comp. Each web image has its original alt text and two captions written by an image captioner, and those three texts are each other's good views.

76
00:05:36,974 --> 00:05:39,008
First finding, sample efficiency.

77
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:54,688
With the same 109 million parameter encoder and the same corpus, good views reach 0.109 on the PRH paper's WIT benchmark after 21.6 million samples, while next token prediction needs 900 million samples to reach 0 .111 42 times more.

78
00:05:54,926 --> 00:05:55,684
One caveat.

79
00:05:56,270 --> 00:06:00,608
Next token prediction was only scored at that 900 million checkpoint.

80
00:06:00,768 --> 00:06:03,408
Second, finding the eye-catching one.

81
00:06:03,568 --> 00:06:10,208
Trained on 900 million samples, the Goodview JEPA scores 0.184 against Dinov2 on WIT.

82
00:06:10,368 --> 00:06:23,008
That's above every language model the authors evaluated, including Quen2572B at 0.154 and Llama 370B at 0.151.

83
00:06:23,246 --> 00:06:26,448
Next token prediction on the same text gets 0.111.

84
00:06:26,638 --> 00:06:32,448
Swap in clips image encoder and it still leads 0 .230 against 0.210.

85
00:06:32,654 --> 00:06:42,048
In the author's words, this model read 116 billion text tokens and beat models 660 times larger, trained on over 100 times more.

86
00:06:42,128 --> 00:06:49,008
They flag caveats, a fourth seed, with a different regularization weight, reached only 0.121.

87
00:06:49,518 --> 00:06:54,128
All four seeds average 0.168, still above every language model.

88
00:06:54,254 --> 00:06:58,208
And the captioner that wrote most of these texts sees images through clips vision encoder.

89
00:06:58,288 --> 00:07:00,800
Third finding, alignment has a scaling law.

90
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:07,420
Mixing good and bad views at different rates, they fit a formula, then predicted 85 new runs before training them.

91
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:22,368
The prediction error was 0.0093, while rerunning one experiment with another seed differs by 0.0096, But extrapolated to 320 million samples, it overestimates the runs with good views.

92
00:07:22,478 --> 00:07:25,728
The authors call it an empirical summary of one recipe.

93
00:07:25,902 --> 00:07:27,328
Two applications close the paper.

94
00:07:27,568 --> 00:07:28,848
First, a captioner.

95
00:07:29,008 --> 00:07:30,688
The decoder is trained on text alone.

96
00:07:30,848 --> 00:07:37,860
To caption an image, embed it with frozen dinof 2 and Dinoff 3, apply one rotation, and decode.

97
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:41,408
Only the rotation is fitted on image dot caption pairs.

98
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:44,608
On Coco, it reaches a cider of 0.838.

99
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:50,208
The clip-based captioners, the paper list report between 0.345 and 0.918.

100
00:07:50,368 --> 00:07:52,964
Second, alignment with no pairs at all.

101
00:07:53,358 --> 00:07:56,288
Finding the rotation from the geometry of the two spaces alone.

102
00:07:56,462 --> 00:08:08,048
On ZeroShot Image Net, 100 top 5 accuracy, the Goodview JPA gets 64.4% versus 47.6 for QN3, 8B rerun under identical conditions.

103
00:08:08,366 --> 00:08:16,528
Clip's text encoder still scores higher at 68.7, while next token prediction on the same texts fails in half its runs.

104
00:08:16,688 --> 00:08:21,248
Putting it together, the original PRH was an observation plus an idealized argument.

105
00:08:21,422 --> 00:08:24,484
This paper gives a provable sufficient condition for JPAs.

106
00:08:24,910 --> 00:08:29,488
Match the positives and the representations agree, up to one rotation.

107
00:08:29,614 --> 00:08:37,248
With the same texts and the same encoder, good views beat next token prediction in all but a few of the comparisons the paper ran.

108
00:08:37,408 --> 00:08:48,848
My read is that the most interesting move here is folding next token prediction into the JEPA framework, which turns language models aligned with vision through scale into a statement about weak positive pairs.

109
00:08:48,942 --> 00:08:53,008
My read is also that this doesn't say next token prediction should be replaced.

110
00:08:53,102 --> 00:08:56,048
The paper measures one thing alignment with vision models.

111
00:08:56,208 --> 00:08:57,488
It doesn't compare generation.

112
00:08:58,446 --> 00:09:00,064
And it doesn't compare reasoning.

113
00:09:00,174 --> 00:09:01,984
The authors list several limitations.

114
00:09:02,158 --> 00:09:04,260
First, the theorems are idealized.

115
00:09:04,622 --> 00:09:12,384
They assume one-to-one renderings, while real modalities are lossy, and no experiment meets the conditions of Theorem 1 exactly.

116
00:09:12,544 --> 00:09:17,584
Second, and the main caveat in their own words where do good views come from?

117
00:09:17,744 --> 00:09:25,344
On web data, two of each image's three texts come from an image captioner, carrying information distilled from image.text training.

118
00:09:25,424 --> 00:09:36,304
Without any captioner, using the alt text and a text-only paraphrase, WIT drops to 0.060 against 0.107 with the recaptions.

119
00:09:36,430 --> 00:09:40,944
So the large-scale comparisons with language models and clip rely on those generated text.

120
00:09:41,184 --> 00:09:45,104
The comparisons between view types on identical text don't.

121
00:09:45,294 --> 00:09:51,104
Third, hyperparameters were tuned only for the good view JPA, not for the bad view and next token baselines.

122
00:09:51,214 --> 00:09:57,412
The authors hope positive views and not scale alone become the lever for building platonic representations.

123
00:09:58,030 --> 00:10:01,584
Extended to modalities like audio, video, and actions.

124
00:10:01,744 --> 00:10:07,104
My read is that the value here is turning a much quoted hypothesis into something you can design for.

125
00:10:07,264 --> 00:10:09,664
If you want alignment, choose the right positives.

126
00:10:09,806 --> 00:10:15,744
My read is also that the most striking numbers depend on a captioner trained on image text data to produce the good views.

127
00:10:15,982 --> 00:10:20,544
For modalities with no paired data to lean on, where good views come from will decide how far this goes.

128
00:10:20,718 --> 00:10:22,384
That's today's Daily Frontier Papers.

129
00:10:22,464 --> 00:10:23,984
The paper is linked in the description.

